\version "2.24.0"
% c459 (Adler part 1 PDF 152, Ex. V-27, and PDF 257, Ex. VIII-25: in a first-inversion chord double the bass toward the bottom and keep the 'open' sound; a third doubled up to the top is 'not good'/'bad'): at 251.1-2 the bass moves to E (I6) under both themes' E, and Hn 4 doubled E4 with Hn 1-2, Hn 3 and the violas, so sixteen notes were E and the chord had only Ob 2's C5 and no G. Hn 4 now goes D4 -> G3 -> C4 (`g2 c'2`, was `e'1`): G3 on beats 1-2, C4 from beat 3 under Hn 3's G4. Hn 3 keeps c072's F4 -> E4 resolution of the seventh. D4 -> C4 directly was rejected (octaves with Ob 2's D5 -> C5); G3 rises to C4 on beat 3 in contrary motion to the bass's E2 -> C2, so there are no fifths into 252 (C4 over F). The score-only force-hshift overrides of c305 at 251 are removed (both horns now have half notes).
% c448 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 51 Ex. 3-6: a note that begins on a weak beat and is held through the strong beat conflicts with the metre and is marked sf; PDF 50: the beginning of a note is heard as stronger than its continuation): 272.2 the IV chord's F and A enter after the accented C on beat 1 and are held into 273; they now carry an accent (Bn 1, Hn 3-4, Vla) so the syncopated subdominant under the allargando is not heard as the decay of the accented C. Notes unchanged.
% c321 (Sebesky PDF 208 Ex. 273, PDF 219, PDF 211-212): 255-258 F2's returning phrase was f in cellos and Hn 3-4 inside ff held brass at its own pitches (Tbn 1-2 G3/E3, Hn 1-2 G3, B.tbn E3, Tpt 3 C4/E4); the violas now join it at the unison (they had repeated C5/E4), the line is ff, and the held brass harmony (Hn 1-2, Tbn 1-2, B.tbn, Tpt 3) is f with a crescendo from 257 to ff at 259, the woodwinds' curve. Tpt 1-2 (chorale top), tuba, harmony and every pitch except the violas' unchanged.
% c182 (A&S Unit 5, PDF 76, 81, 83): 252 last eighth of the P line E6 (was G6: G-F octaves with the S head into 253); 249 Cb F1 (was F2, a seventh down to G1); 249.3 Ob 2 and Tpt 2 A4 (was B4 against Tpt 3's C5); 261 Ob 2 keeps C5 (was A flat 4: the doubled seventh C of the half-diminished 6/5 fell C-A flat in octaves with Hn 4); C is the Ger6's common tone, F4 at 262.
% c129 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 112: in difficult cases mark the wood-wind one degree louder than the brass; Ex. 166, PDF 335: the hymn's ff statement has full wood-wind ff over ff brass): 257 the wood-winds re-enter f (were mf) and reach ff at 259 (were f), still after their rest at 255-256 and still growing to fff at 263, so the crescendo by addition (c033, c038) is kept one level up and the flutes' top octave of the chorale line is heard over the ff trumpets.
% c128 (expression; Adler ch. 11, PDF 38-40: Tchaikovsky 4/i breaks the climax off to the low strings before the next tutti; ch. 15, PDF 190: a loud chord cut off over a line already sounding; PDF 192: seams must not show): 264 the harmony winds and brass and the timpani release on beat 1, the woodwinds and Tpt 1-2 (chorale top line, length kept) diminuendo through 264, Vc/Cb diminuendo on the held C, so the strings' scale figure (bars 3-4 in C major) is heard as at bars 3-4; 257-258 Vln II, Vla, Vc, Cb crescendo to 259's ff with the winds; 247 Cbsn ff with the tuba it doubles; 277 B.cl, Cbsn, B.tbn hairpins end at their rest.
% c126 (playability; Adler ch. 6-7, PDF 188: Brahms 1/iv dovetails the flutes so one breathes while the other holds, 'without diminishing the intensity'; PDF 175 and 198: oboists must be given frequent rests; PDF 230: low contrabassoon notes take much breath, give periodic rests; PDF 198: C6-F6 on the oboe is thin and hard to control): 257-264 the woodwind (and brass) chorale held 8 (10) bars mf<fff with no breath; staggered breath marks at tie ends: Fl 1 after 258 and 261, Cl 1-2, Cbsn, Tpt 1-2, Tbn 1-2, tuba after 258; Ob 1-2, B.cl., Bn 1-2, Hn 1-2, B.tbn after 260 (Vln I, Tpt 3, Hn 3-4 and the other group cover each gap). Fl 2's arpeggio 257-262 drops its last eighth at 259 and 261 (Vln II keeps the figure) so it can breathe before the fff E5 at 263. Cbsn and tuba breathe after 250. Ob 1 rests 265-268 (G5-E6 leap ff in the thin register, head kept by Fl 1-2, Cl 1, Vln I-II; the oboe had played 247-264 with two bars' rest) and re-enters a2 with Ob 2 at 269.
% c123 (form and pacing; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 118: a recurring idea is varied by alteration of details; PDF 125-126: the last note of one part leads into the first of the next; PDF 116, 133: the harmonic basis is set below the melody in dynamic power; PDF 130: a long crescendo is made by adding instruments; PDF 136: after a long rest the tuba re-enters pp or ff): 268 Fl 1-2 and Vln I continue E-D-C-B into 269's C6 (the top line no longer repeats 265-266 with a three-beat hole); 265 the accompaniment (Tpt 3, tuba, timpani, Cb) f under the ff themes, Tpt 3, tuba and timpani crescendo 269-270 to ff at 271, Cb stays f 265-274 (no ff-to-f drop at the Largamente); 247 tuba ff (re-entry after 235-246).
% c122 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 268: outer-voice frames without imperfect consonances 'might sound empty unless decorated'; PDF 267: voice-leading chords carry repeated patterns; Unit 21, PDF 368: over a tonic pedal I-IV-V7-I is the usual succession and the upper parts' dissonances resolve normally): 272 the F1 peak A-G-F had only octaves over the C pedal (beat 4 a bare F over C; Tpt 3 G under the A). Now IV6/4 under it from beat 2: Hn 3 A4, Hn 4 F4, Bn 1 F4, violas div. F4-A4, and Tpt 3 A4 for the bar; into 273 every inner F steps to E (Hn 4, Bn 1, Vla) and every A to G (Tpt 3, Vla), Hn 3 A4 to C5. The violas' "div." moves from 273 to 272.
% c117 (texture; Adler ch. 15, PDF 179 and 181: a middleground figuration under a foreground theme is given mixed colour and mass, e.g. Scheherazade's 'dance figure' in piccolo, flutes and both violin sections, Holst's figuration in winds and violins): 257-262 Fl 2 doubles Vln II's arpeggio figure an octave higher (was held harmony C6-A flat5-F5, all still in Vln II, Ob 2 and the brass), so the figure is heard under the ff brass chorale.
% c117 (Adler ch. 15, PDF 184: each fragment is given to a part that is clearly heard, and a held part does not sit on the moving voice's pitches): 275 Vln II plays E5 only (unis., was div. C5-E5 tremolo), so the scherzo quotation's lower octave (Tpt 1-2, Cl 1: C5-G4-G4-C5-E5) is not absorbed by a C5 tremolo at its own pitch; C5 stays held in Hn 3.
% c087 (lens 7; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 75 and 79): Cl 2 joins Ob 1/Cl 1 on the S head upper octave 251-254 (two horns below need three winds above in forte); Cl 2 and Bn 1 double F1 at 265-268 (Tpt + Cl + Vla over Hn + Bn + Vc).
% ALLEGRO MOLTO, bars 247-279 (c070: v01 numbers in this paragraph and the next; v01 243-262 = now 247-279 after c013/c023; the Maestoso of v01 251-255 was removed in c013, the last chorale is 255-264 at Più allegro 2 = 88 since v76; iiø6/5 with A♭ at 259-260 (c080: was described as ♭VI), Ger6 at 261, V7-I 262-263) (cyclic-notes 'Owed' items 6, 7, 3). 243-250: movement I's first-theme head in C major on top (flute 1 and
% violins I, oboe 1/clarinet 1/violins II, violas, in octaves) combined with movement I's second-theme head in the brass (trumpets, horns),
% fast, ff, over the hammered double-bass eighths: movement I's tutti layout turned to C major. 251-255: Maestoso, the chorale's last
% statement in full brass, ending C-B-C, over I6 - vi - iiø6/5 (borrowed) - bVI - V7 - I: the symphony's first root-position V7-I in C since
% movement I bar 135; trumpet 1 takes B to C, trumpet 2 has the E natural (the Don Giovanni arrival of cyclic-notes). 256-262: tonic close,
% movement I's timpani pulse, last chord ff. c003: every part 20 bars; 260 echoes the cadence (V on the eighth, C-B-C in the violins), final chord 261, fermata 262.

% c023 (form lens; A&S Unit 32): the C-major close after the last cadence (262-263) was 9 bars (263-271, about 11 s). Eight bars added, 269-276
% (old 269-271 are now 277-279): 269-272 the two heads exchanged (this movement's F1 head on top in Fl/Ob/Cl/Tpt 1-2/violins, movement I's head
% below in Hn 1-2, Bn 1, violas, cellos), mirroring 265-268; 273-276 IV6/4 - iv6/4 - I over the C pedal with C held on top (common tone in the
% soprano, A&S PDF 587) and the inner line A - A flat - G: the bar-1 motion A flat -> G (German sixth -> V) restated at the tonic level
% (A&S PDF 600-601, motive enlarged into the plan; PDF 592-593, flat VI in major). No new V-I before 277. fff only at 278 (was 270).
% c032 (harmony; open item from the scherzo critic, decided in cycle 30): 273-275 complete the chain of the closes of movements II and III
% (E flat-D-D flat-C in II, A flat; B-A sharp-A-G sharp over E - A sharp dim7 - A minor - E in III): G-F sharp-F-E over
% C (273, first half) - F sharp dim7 over the C pedal (273, second half) - F minor 6/4 (274) - C (275), in Hn 1-2 a2, Tpt 3, Ob 2, Vln II and
% Tbn 2; inner lines C-A-A flat-G (keeps the A-A flat-G of c023) and E-E flat-F-E (violas, Hn 4, Bn 1). The quotation at 275-276 then
% continues the line E-D-D flat-C, so 273-276 run 5-#4-4-3-2-b2-1. Voicing as the scherzo's coda 365-368, a major third lower.
% c034 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, PDF 151: weight the octave the listener must hear as the line): 273-276 Vln I lower note and Ob 1 join the chain G-F sharp-F-E
% (were A-A flat-G, the line already in Fl 2, Cl 2, Tpt 2, Tbn 1, violas), so the chain is the strongest moving line under the held C6. 257 Fl/Ob/Cl re-enter mf
% (were p under ff brass and strings: Adler PDF 156, a rest before a colour enters is wasted if the entry cannot be heard).
% c032: 247 Vc and Bn 1 G4 on beat 3 (were E4: E-D under Tpt 2's B-A, parallel 5ths).
% c036 (playability; Adler ch. 6-7, PDF 175, 198, 230: wind and brass players need periodic rests): Hn 3-4 rest 265-268 (Tbn 1-2 keep the doubling under F1),
% Hn 1-2 rest 269-272 (Hn 3-4, Tbn 1, Bn 1, violas keep movement I's head), so no horn plays 243-279 without a break; Bn 2 rests 247-254 (it doubled the bass trombone at the unison);
% 257 B.cl, Bn 1-2, Cbn enter mf with the upper winds (Adler PDF 185, 210: a weak entry under ff brass is covered); 278 B.cl C3 (low C extension not required, PDF 216).
% c038 (expression; Casanelles ch. 4, PDF 148, 152): 259 the wood-wind crescendo from 257 is staged at f where strings and brass begin theirs; 273 Tpt 1-2 restate ff after their rests at 271-272; 273-279 Largamente (global.ily).
% c048 (expression; A&S Unit 3, PDF 50: the start of a note is stronger than its continuation; PDF 53: bar 2 of a four-bar group is weak): 273-274 the held C6/C5 on top (Fl 1, Tpt 1, Hn 3) is tied and the timpani roll is one tied roll 273-276 (c045 rule);
% the pedal re-strikes at 274 without accent, so the moving chain (F sharp -> F, A -> A flat) carries the accent of 274. 255-262 Hn 3 and Tpt 3 accent only the first half note of each bar (were accents on every beat, which removes the strong-weak pattern of the metre, PDF 50).
% c049 (holistic, Dvorak 7/iv; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 157, 166, Ex. 309-310 PDF 499-500: crossing only for a short, deliberate emphasis; a doubling is redistributed when the
% register changes): 247-254 Vln II up an octave with Vln I and Fl 1-2, Ob 1-2 on the trumpets' S head (Ob 1 = Tpt 1, Ob 2 = Tpt 2): at G4-A5 the P head's lower octave
% crossed the S head in Tpt 1-2 and made seconds with it (247 D flat5/C5, 249 F5/E5, 250 D5/E5, 251 B4/C5), the defect c047 removed at 265-268.
% c371 (melody lens; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 54: a melody in the middle of the orchestral range is not prominent by position and needs doubling; PDF 61 h: middle-range melodies go to the violins in octaves with violas and cellos; PDF 63 note: an unequal octave distribution does not matter): 255-258 Vln II plays F2's tonic-major phrase an octave above the violas, cellos and Hn 3-4 (G4-G5) instead of the arpeggio, which it resumes at 259 with Cl 1 (257); F2's notes unchanged.
% c049 (Dvorak 7/iv 410-415, corpus PDF 202: the tune in long notes over arpeggiated eighths in Vln II/Vla/Vc; Rimsky-Korsakov Ex. 291, PDF 469-470, the tutti form of a
% sung melody with running middle strings): 255-262 Vln II break the chorale's chords into eighths (were whole notes doubling the held brass), below the chorale's top line.
% c056 (Adler part 1 PDF 30): 273 Vln I, Vln II and violas marked div. (upper and lower lines of the chain; as double stops the held C6 over G5-F sharp5-F5 and C5 under G5-F sharp5 need one finger in two places).
% c078 (expression; Adler ch. 7, PDF 187: a tie or slur is one breath; PDF 188: give the flutist time to rest after sustained passages; PDF 198: oboe breath control makes rests mandatory): 264 winds and brass release the fff cadence chord on beat 4 (c2. r4, was a whole note tied into the 265 attacks), strings and timpani hold; 272 Fl, Ob, Cl breathe with the trumpets at the end of the allargando phrase.
% c079 (lens 9, Beethoven 3/iv: the Presto, bar 431, brings back the movement's opening run while the winds hold ff; Adler part 2 PDF 224: Ravel returns the opening colours at the end;
% PDF 213: Bizet's scale before the repeat anticipates the new scoring): 263-264 Vln I, Vln II, Vla play the bar 3-4 scale figure in C major after the cadence chord, launching 265.
% c083 (A&S Unit 15, PDF 237 Ex. 15-10b: I-(III) with 8-7-6 in an inner voice; PDF 239: III instead of a repeated I6 "when the stepwise descent 8-7-6 ... is wanted, or when
% excessive repetitions of 1 in any of the upper voices would otherwise result"; PDF 236 Ex. 15-9: III-VI-II-V-I): 256, the second bar of the chorale's G over the E bass, is iii (E-G-B)
% instead of a second bar of I6: Hn 4 C4-B3-A3 (255-257, 8-7-6), Vln II arpeggio with B, Tpt 3 E4 (was C4), violas E4 one bar early (were C5, eight repeated tonics).
% The protected chords (I6 255, vi, iiø6/5, Ger6, V7, I) and the chorale line are unchanged; 255-262 becomes I6-iii-vi-iiø6/5-Ger6-V7, a descending-fifth chain into the cadence.
% c085 (engraving; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38-39: a slur is one bow, and the louder the passage the fewer notes per bow): 263-264 the scale figure at fff is bowed 4+2+4 (c''8( b'8) | c''8( ...)) instead of 4+6 across the barline (Vln I, Vln II, Vla); 277 cellos 'div.' on C3-G3 (PDF 30: without div. the chord is a double-stop), as violins and violas are marked at 278.
% c088 (expression; Adler ch. 10, PDF 303 Ex. X-5 and PDF 318 Ex. X-46: brilliance and loudness rise with register; PDF 305 Ex. X-13, Mahler 1: f, ff, then cresc. to fff, the fff kept for the peak): 263 Fl 1 takes the chorale's last C two octaves above Tpt 1 (C7, as at 223) and Cl 2 plays 257-264 an octave higher (E5-F5-E flat5-G5-G5, out of the throat register), so the 263 chord is no longer an octave lower on top than 223 and 278; 255 Vln II, Vla, Vc, Cb f (Vln I stays ff with the chorale) and ff at 259 with the existing crescendo, so the accompaniment grows f-ff-fff like the winds (mf 257, f 259); 265 Cl 1's ff on the eighth rest, merged with Cl 2's ff (the two marks an eighth apart printed as 'ffff').
% c121 (melody; Adler ch. 3, PDF 74 Ex. III-4, Brahms 1/iv: the tune's opening bars return only after a continuation, and the restatement changes its third bar;
% this score's own exposition 11-16 answers the head with the climb and then with the head a third higher): 267-268 F1's head moves up a third (E-B-C | D-C-B-A-G
% in Tpt 1-2, Cl 2 at C5; Hn 1-2, Bn 1, Vla, Vc at C4), so the head is not heard three times at one pitch in 265-270; Tpt 3 G at 267 and F at 268 (I, then ii7 over the C pedal).
% 272 the major F1 takes its consequent's peak bar (A-G-F, as A flat-G-F at 22 and 162) instead of the antecedent's F-E-D, so its last statement reaches A6/A5;
% the seventh F resolves to E in Cl 1 at 273 (the others fall to C as before).
% c184 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 51: trumpet = trombone = 2 horns in forte, wood-wind half a horn, one string department two winds): 247 Tbn 1-2 f (were ff) on the P head's lower octave, the level of Tpt 1-2 on the S head (c084), so the two trombones three octaves under the flutes and violins no longer outweigh both heads' upper voices.
% c184 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 83-84: the bass of an inverted seventh chord is not doubled in the upper parts; PDF 85: the harmonic notes must not be lacking in the upper parts): 259-260 Hn 3 A flat 3 (was F4, the iio6/5's bass F, already in Tpt 3 and violas), so the borrowed A flat has two brass (Tbn 2, Hn 3) instead of one; Hn 3 rises A flat 3 -> E flat 4 at 261 (Tbn 2 falls A flat 3 -> E flat 3, contrary). Pitch classes, top line and 261-263 unchanged.
% c186 (playability; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38 [Vc 248 mark set below: above it triggers a LilyPond spacing error]: louder and heavier toward the frog, a composer marks bow direction only to counteract the players' habit; Ex. II-19: two up-bows in a row so the accent falls on a down-bow; PDF 42: down-bows retaken with the bow lifted): 247-254 Vln I, Vln II, Vc: down-bow on 247's pickup (retaken in the eighth rest), hooked up-bows at 248 b8 and 252 e8, so the head's accents (247 E6, 248 A5, 249-251, 252-253 A6, Vln 254 G6) fall on down-bows; by plain alternation 247, 252, 253, 254 were up-bows. Pitches, slurs and dynamics unchanged.
% c186 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 25: a wind or brass player must stop to breathe; c126's staggered breaths left Hn 3-4 none, and Hn 4 plays 235-263 without a rest): Hn 3-4 breathe after 250 (with Hn 1-2, Cbsn, tuba) and after 256 (no other part breathes there; Hn 1-2, Tpt, Tbn hold).
% c274 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 87: short arco chords sound well only forte and are given to Vln I, Vln II and violas as double notes, divided between them for ease and resonance, open and gut strings the most powerful): 277 the two short ff chords are double stops 'non div.' in Vln I (E5/C6, D5/B5), Vln II (E4/C5, D4/B4) and violas (G3/E4, G3/D4 on open strings), so every player strikes both notes (was 'div.', each note half the section); 'div. a 3' at 278 for the held chord as before (PDF 88). Cellos stay div. (PDF 87: the cello takes the low note with the basses).
% c318 (expression; Adler ch. 2, PDF 38: an upbeat is taken up-bow unless marked, a crescendo lies on the up-bow, heavier strokes at the frog; Ex. II-19 and II-20, Coriolanus: down-bow then up-bow on a long note so the next ff attack is a down-bow; PDF 39: the cello bow is shorter): bow signs only. 259 Vc down-up on the two-bar ff crescendo F (was one down-bow, which also put 262 G on a down-bow before 263's marked down-bow); 263 Vln I down-bow on the fff C after 'change bow at will'; 267 Vln I-II and 269, 271 Vla-Vc down-bow on the pickup into the movement-I head, as at 247; 267 Vla-Vc and 269 Vln I down-bow on the accented F1 head, which alternation put on an up-bow (avoid-slur 'ignore: with the score's stem-side Script offset, a second script on a slur's first half note otherwise removed the slur).
% c359 (Adler PDF 243, Ex. VIII-11: violins as an agitated pedal while winds, brass, violas and cellos carry the thematic material; PDF 161, Ex. V-37: upper strings tremolo ff on held tones under a tune in the lower instruments; PDF 261-262, Ex. VIII-33/34: one colour kept for a recurring idea): 255-262 Vln I keeps the chorale's top line (G5-E6-D6-C6-B5, Fl 1 and Tpt 1-2) as bowed tremolo, the colour the chorale had at 223-225; pitches, ff, hairpin and the 263 entry unchanged.
% c384 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov, appendix of single tutti chords, PDF 505-507: every held f-fff tutti chord with divided or doubled strings has them tremolando (chords 1, 2, 4, 9-12, 17, 19, 20; only chords 3 and 18b are struck short); ch. III, PDF 88: sustained chords 'or tremolando passages, often used as a substitute', forte tremolando on two strings): 278-279.1 the final chord's strings (Vln I, Vln II, violas div. a 3, cellos div., basses) play it bowed tremolo, as they play the same fff C-major chord at the arrival (223) and the closing chain 273-276; pitches, divisi, dynamics, the cut on 279.2 and every other part unchanged.
finaleVlnOne = { r8 g''8\downbow(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8\upbow( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0) Beam c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) e'''8\upbow( d'''8) a'''4.-> e'''8 | f'''8( g'''8 a'''4.->) \once \offset positions #'(0.25 . -0.25) Beam g'''8( f'''8 e'''8) | g'''2.-> r4 | g''1:32\ff~ | g''1:32 | e'''1:32~ | e'''1:32 | d'''1:32\<~ | d'''1:32 | c'''1:32 | b''1:32 | c'''4\fff\downbow g''8( f''8 e''8 d''8) c''8( b'8) | c''8( d''8 e''8 f''8) g''4-> g'4-> | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8\downbow( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) e'''8( d'''8 c'''4 b''4) | c'''2->-\tweak avoid-slur #'ignore \downbow( g''4. a''8 | bes''4-.) a''8( g''8 f''4-.) e''4-. | d''4( g''8 a''8 b''4 d'''4) | a'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) | <g'' c'''>2:32->\ff^\markup \italic "div." <fis'' c'''>2:32 | <f'' c'''>1:32-> | <e'' c'''>4->\< g''8-.^\markup \italic "unis." g''8-. c'''4-. e'''4->~( | e'''4 d'''8 des'''8 c'''4-.) r4 | <e'' c'''>4->^\markup \italic "non div." r8 <d'' b''>8 <e'' c'''>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <c''' e''' g'''>1:32->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <c''' e''' g'''>4:32 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c101 (melody lens; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 60, distributions 2 and 3, Ex. 27 Sadko: a quick forte figure goes to Vns I+II over the violas for brilliance in the upper part; Vn I over Vn II+violas is for a fuller cantabile lower part): 263-264 Vln II joins Vln I on the scale figure (G5-B4, then up to G5), violas alone take the lower octave, so the opening run is heard over the fff wind and brass hold.
% c118 (Adler ch. 2, PDF 37: notes under one slur are one bow): 276 Vln II E-D-D flat-C on one bow, as the oboes it doubles and Vln I (was e''4 d''8( des''8) c''4-.).
% c376 (playability; Adler ch. 3, adler1 PDF 74, Ex. III-5, Tchaikovsky 5/ii: the G string is the thickest and most sonorous and grows intense in its higher positions; PDF 75: the D string is the least powerful;
%   PDF 76: a passage meant for one string is marked sul G): 255-257 Vln II's F2 an octave above violas and cellos lay mostly on the D string (G4-E4-D4, only C4 on the G string) under the ff brass chorale; now sul G up to D5 at 257, crossing at 258's G5.
% c436 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 26: on the three lower strings the highest note is the one in the fourth position, the octave or ninth of the open string; c376's "sul G" (Adler PDF 74-76) took C5 and D5 at 257 to the seventh and eighth positions of the G string at ff, Allegro molto): the "sul G" line now ends on 256's G4, the octave of the open string; 257's C5-D5 are taken on the D string. Notes, slurs and dynamics unchanged.
finaleVlnTwo = { r8 g''8\downbow(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8\upbow( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0) Beam c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) e'''8\upbow( d'''8) a'''4.-> e'''8 | f'''8( g'''8 a'''4.->) \once \offset positions #'(0.25 . -0.25) Beam g'''8( f'''8 e'''8) | g'''2.-> r4 | \once \override TextSpanner.bound-details.left.text = \markup \italic "sul G" g'2.->(\ff^\markup \italic "espress."\startTextSpan e'4) | d'4( c'4 e'4 g'4\stopTextSpan) | c''2.( d''4) | g''4.( f''8 e''4 d''4) | aes'8(\ff\< d'' f'' aes'') f''( d'' aes' f') | aes'8( d'' f'' aes'') f''( d'' aes' d'') | aes'8( c'' es'' fis'') aes''( fis'' es'' c'') | g'8( b' d'' f'') g''( f'' d'' b') | <e'' g''>4\fff g''8( f''8 e''8 d''8) c''8( b'8) | c''8( d''8 e''8 f''8) g''4-> g'4-> | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8\downbow( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | c''2->( g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | d'4( g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) | <c'' g''>2:32->\ff^\markup \italic "div." <c'' fis''>2:32 | <c'' f''>1:32-> | e''1:32\<^\markup \italic "unis." | e''4( d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | <e' c''>4->^\markup \italic "non div." r8 <d' b'>8 <e' c''>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <c'' e'' g''>1:32->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <c'' e'' g''>4:32 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c040 (cyclic lens; Adler ch. 1, PDF 19: timbre clarifies form and content; ch. 15, PDF 188-189: at-pitch doubling with an instrument of affinity): 273-276 the violas no longer
% move E4 -> E flat 4 (movement II bar 2's undoing of the thread, at the same pitch, after the thread closed in the violas at 219-220). Upper violas take the
% chain G4-F sharp4-F4-E4 with Hn 1-2 and Tpt 3, so the chain's 3 lands on E4, the violas' E of movement I bar 182 and finale 220; lower violas take C-A-A flat-G (A3-A flat3-G3) with Tbn 1.
% Every chord keeps its pitch classes (E flat at 273 stays in Hn 4 and Bn 1).
% c109 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 79: string and brass in unison never blend, each is heard separately; the pairs that match are Violin + Trumpet, Viola + Horn, 'Cello + Trombone; PDF 79-80: Violas/'Cellos + Cl + Horn is a natural three-group unison): 265-268 the violas play F1 at C4 with Hn 1-2, Bn 1 and the cellos instead of at C5 under two ff trumpets; the C5 octave is Tpt 1-2 + Cl 2. The scale figure at 264 now ends on G3 and steps to C4.
finaleVla = { e'2.~(\ff e'8 d'8) | c'4( a'2-- g'4) | f'4.( e'8 d'4 f'8 a'8) | c''2( b'2) | e'2.~( e'8 d'8) | c'4( a'2-- g'4) | f'4.( e'8 d'4 c'4) | b2( c'2) | g2.->(\ff^\markup \italic "espress." e4) | d4( c4 e4 g4) | c'2.( d'4) | g'4.( f'8 e'4 d'4) | f'4\< f' f' f' | f'4 f' f' f' | fis'4 fis' fis' fis' | g'4 g' g' g' | e'4\fff g'8( f'8 e'8 d'8) c'8( b8) | c'8( d'8 e'8 f'8) g'4-> g4-> | c'2->(\ff g4. a8 | bes4-.) a8( g8 f4-.) e4-. | e'2->-\tweak avoid-slur #'ignore \downbow( b4. c'8 | d'4-.) c'8( b8 a4-.) g4-. | r8 g8_\downbow( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) d'2.-> | r8 g8_\downbow( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) <f' a'>2.->^\markup \italic "div." | <e' g'>2:32->\ff <a fis'>2:32 | <aes f'>1:32-> | <g e'>1:32\< | <g e'>1:32 | <g e'>4->^\markup \italic "non div." r8 <g d'>8 <g e'>4-> r4 | \once \textLengthOn <g e' c''>1:32->\fff~^\markup \italic "div. a 3" | <g e' c''>4:32 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c026: Tpt 1-2 rest at 271-272 (playability, brass endurance: about 90 s of ff/fff from 243 with three C6s; the fourth F1 statement stays in Fl/Ob/Cl/violins).
% c017: 247-254 violas double horns 1-2 on movement I's second-theme head (each theme gets a string colour, Adler II PDF 178); held brass, tuba, contrabassoon and Tpt 3 f under the two ff heads (PDF 184). 255-262 violas in repeated quarters under the chorale (PDF 181).
% c010: 254 first half Vla and Tpt 3 F sharp: the flat VI becomes the German sixth A flat-C-E flat-F sharp of bar 1, resolving to V7-I (A&S PDF 593; cyclic-notes: movement I bars 10-13 enlarged).
% c127 (texture; Adler ch. 17, PDF 274: keep accompanying forces registrally distant from the principal line; PDF 260: the figuration lies under the solo; PDF 279: Bizet doubles above the voice only at the climax): 257-262 Fl 2 joins Fl 1 on the chorale top line (breath after 260, dovetailed with Fl 1 after 258 and 261, so the top flute octave never stops; Adler2 PDF 188); its arpeggio, which peaked a fourth above the chorale (A6, A flat 6, G6), goes to Cl 1 at Vln II's pitch (mixed colour kept, c117), below the line. 257-258 violas C4 with Hn 3 and Tpt 3 instead of re-striking the chorale's E4 (c074).
% c333 (form and pacing; Adler part 2 ch. 17, PDF 288, Ex. XVII-24: in the second phrase Haydn's flute leaves the soprano tune and doubles the tenor two octaves higher, 'devices like these add great variety'; PDF 279: the upper octave is added where the line peaks; PDF 257, 261: foreground roles assigned to both lines):
%   251-254 Fl 1-2 a2 leave movement I's first-theme head (still in Vln I-II at the same pitch, and Bn 1, Vc, Tbn 1-2 below) and take the second-theme head's second phrase two octaves above Hn 1-2/violas (E6-A6-C6),
%   so the combination does not thin when Tpt 1-2 leave the second-theme head at 251 (they rest for the chorale at 255); the flutes still rest 255-256 and take the chorale's E6 at 257.
finaleFluteOne = { r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0 . -0.25) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | \once \offset positions #'(-0.25 . 0) Beam c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | e'''2.~( e'''8 d'''8 | c'''4 a'''2-- g'''4 | f'''4. e'''8 d'''4 c'''4 | b''2 c'''2) \breathe | R1*2 | \partCombineApart e'''1\f\<~ | e'''1 \breathe | d'''1\ff\<~ | d'''1 | c'''1 \breathe | b''1 | \partCombineAutomatic c'''1\fff~ | c'''2.\> r4\! | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) \once \override Beam.positions = #'(0.25 . -0.25) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) e'''8( d'''8 c'''4 b''4) | c'''2->( g''4. a''8 | bes''4-.) a''8( g''8 f''4-.) e''4-. | d''4( g''8 a''8 b''4 d'''4) | a'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) \breathe | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #1.2 c'''1->~ | c'''1 | c'''4->\< g''8-. g''8-. c'''4-. e'''4->~( | e'''4 d'''8 des'''8 c'''4-.) r4 | c'''4-> r8 b''8 c'''4-> r4 | g'''1->\fff~ | g'''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c404 (orchestration; Adler part 2 ch. 15, PDF 159 rule 2: pitches in the instrument's best register for the dynamic; PDF 160, Missa Solemnis m. 21: in the full chord the flute doubles Vln I at pitch; PDF 165: unison of like instruments adds weight; PDF 162, Schumann: the voice leading 'according to the book'):
%   263-264 Fl 2 resolves its leading tone B5 to C6 with Fl 1 (a2 on the chorale's last C, at pitch with Vln I) instead of leaping down a fifth to E5, the flute's weak register at fff; E5 stays in Vln II, E4 in Ob 2, Hn 3, Tpt 3 and the violas. Harmony, the cut at 264.1 and every other part unchanged.
finaleFluteTwo = { r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) d'''8( des'''8 c'''8 | c'''4--) b''8( c'''8) a''4.-> g''8 | r8 g''8( f'''4.->) e'''8( d'''8 c'''8) | c'''8-. d'''-. e'''-. d'''-. g'''4.-> f'''8 | e'''2.~( e'''8 d'''8 | c'''4 a'''2-- g'''4 | f'''4. e'''8 d'''4 c'''4 | b''2 c'''2) \breathe | R1*2 | e'''1\f\<~ | e'''1 | d'''1\ff\<~ | d'''1 \breathe | c'''1 | b''1 | c'''1\fff~ | c'''2.\> r4\! | r8 g''8(\ff e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) e'''8( d'''8 c'''4 b''4) | c'''2->( g''4. a''8 | bes''4-.) a''8( g''8 f''4-.) e''4-. | d''4( g''8 a''8 b''4 d'''4) | a'''2-> g'''4( f'''4) \breathe | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 c'''2-> a''2 | aes''1-> | g''1~\< | g''1 | c'''4-> r8 b''8 c'''4-> r4 | e'''1->\fff~ | e'''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c090 (cyclic lens; Adler ch. 17, PDF 274: keep accompanying parts registrally distant from the solo line; PDF 288: Haydn moves a doubling to follow an inner line): 276 Ob 1-2 and Vln II
%   no longer hold E5 against the scherzo quotation's tail E5-D5-D flat5-C5 (Tpt 1-2, Cl 1); they continue the chain G-F sharp-F-E into it, so 5-#4-4-3-2-b2-1 sounds in one voice.
% c266 (Sebesky PDF 71, 79-80: oboes in unison are harsh and the lowest fifth F4-B flat3 is coarse; octave couplings blend better): 269-271 Ob 1 takes F1 an octave higher with Fl 1-2 and Vln I (top D6), so the oboes are in octaves and only Ob 2 goes down to E4/D4; 272 Ob 1 back on A5.
% c274 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. III, PDF 90 rules 3-4: concords, not fourths or fifths, within one colour, 'specially ... the oboe'; corresponding registers; PDF 95-96: mixed unisons of doubled timbres): 277 Ob 1 E5-D5-E5 over Ob 2 C5-B4-C5 (a third; was C5 over E4-D4-E4, Ob 2 in its coarse low register); 278 Ob 2 E5 under Ob 1 C6 (a sixth, Ob 2 in unison with Cl 1; was G5, a fourth in the oboe pair). Harmony, rhythm and dynamics unchanged.
finaleOboeOne = { e''2.~(\ff e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 f''8 a''8 | c'''2 b''2) \breathe | e''2.~( e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 c''4 | b'2 c''2) \breathe | R1*2 | e''1\f\<~ | e''1 | d''1\ff\<~ | d''1 \breathe | c''1 | b'1 | c''1\fff~ | c''2.\> r4\! | R1*4 | c'''2->(\ff g''4. a''8 | bes''4-.) a''8( g''8 f''4-.) e''4-. | d''4( g''8 a''8 b''4 d'''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | g''2-> fis''2 | f''1-> | e''1\< | e''4( d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | e''4-> r8 d''8 e''4-> r4 | c'''1->\fff~ | c'''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
finaleOboeTwo = { c''2.~(\ff c''8 b'8 | a'4 f''2-- e''4 | d''4. c''8 a'4 d''8 f''8 | e''2 d''2) \breathe | c''2.~( c''8 b'8 | a'4 f''2-- e''4 | d''4. c''8 b'4 a'4 | g'2 a'2) \breathe | R1*2 | c''1\f\<~ | c''1 | c''1\ff\<~ | c''1 \breathe | c''1 | f'1 | e'1\fff~ | e'2.\> r4\! | R1*4 | c''2->(\ff g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | d'4( g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | g''2-> fis''2 | f''1-> | e''1\< | e''4( d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | c''4-> r8 b'8 c''4-> r4 | e''1->\fff~ | e''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c019: 247-254 Cl 1-2 join horns 1-2 and violas on movement I's second-theme head (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 79: violas + clarinet + horn, the natural three-group unison; PDF 67: not all wood-wind on one line), so the first-theme head keeps flutes, oboes and violins above the brass register
% c044 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, PDF 164 Mahler 1 and PDF 165: high brass doubled an octave higher by the upper winds; at-pitch doubling loses colour; PDF 161 Weber: oboe, not clarinet, with the trumpet): 257-264 Cl 1 an octave higher (E6-D6-C6-B5-C6) with Fl 1 and Vln I; Ob 1 stays with Tpt 1. The 263 cadence chord no longer has four wood-winds inside the trumpets' unisons.
% c047 (texture; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 106: wood-wind and brass combine like melodic lines; PDF 108: in a chain of chords the stationary parts go to the brass, the moving parts to the wood-wind; PDF 110-111: wood-wind form the harmonic part above the trumpets):
% 265-268 Ob 1-2, Cl 1-2 and Vln II take movement I's head an octave higher (G5-E6, with Fl and Vln I): at G4-E5 it crossed F1 in Tpt 1-2, Hn 1-2 and violas and made seconds with it (C5 against B flat4 at 266, 268; B4 against A4 at 265, 267).
% 273-274 Cl 1 leaves the held C5 (already in Tpt 1 and Hn 3) for the inner line E-E flat-F (Hn 4, Bn 1) an octave higher, so each closing-chain line has a wood-wind above the brass; pitch classes unchanged.
% c077 (texture; Adler part 1 ch. 8, PDF 252, 257, 265-268): 247-254 Cl 1 doubles the S head an octave above the violas, at the pitch of Ob 1 and Tpt 1
% (Dvorak Carnival, PDF 267-268: clarinets fill out the upper octave by doubling the violas an octave higher), so that at 253-254, when Tpt 1-2 rest,
% the upper line still has two carriers (Ob 1 + Cl 1) instead of one oboe; Cl 2 stays with the violas and horns on E4 (was Cl 1-2 a2 on E4).
% c109 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 67: in octaves the natural order is flute or oboe above clarinet; a clarinet above the oboe confuses the registers; Ex. 64, PDF 235: oboes and clarinet together under the flutes): 257-264 the chorale's clarinet leaves E6 (where it lay an octave above Ob 1, since c044) and plays the line at E5 with Ob 1 and Tpt 1-2 (PDF 74-75: trumpet + oboe + clarinet, finer legato); the upper octave is Fl 1 + Vln I. To avoid a crossing on the shared staff the line goes to Cl 2 and Cl 1 takes Cl 2's harmony notes (E5-F5-E flat 5-G5); same pitches otherwise.
% c379 (holistic; Adler part 2 ch. 16, PDF 205 and 208, Ex. XVI-9 mm. 5-6, 9-12: in Brahms's Hungarian Dance 1 the flutes play the arpeggio while the clarinets 'flutter' in the opposite direction on the implied harmony notes; PDF 229: like lines at the same pitch absorb each other; PDF 213: Bizet's dovetailed runs):
%   259-262 Cl 1 no longer doubles Vln II's arpeggio in unison; it breaks the same chords in contrary motion (A flat5 down to A flat4 while Vln II rises, and back), so the two parts meet and part on every half bar and the clarinet is heard as its own line. Same chord tones, range F4-G5 (below the chorale top line, c127);
%   258's last eighth is E5 (was C5), so the line steps E5-F5 into 259 (C5-F5 made octaves with Tpt 3's C4-F4, an A flat5 start octaves with Tbn 2's C-A flat); 259-260 start on F5, 260 ends on D5 with Vln II (an F5 there made octaves with Tpt 3 and violas F-F sharp at 261); breaths at the ends of 259 and 261 as before; 261's F sharp5 rises to G5 at 262.1 after the breath; 262 ends on G5 into the fff G5 at 263. Vln II, Cl 2, harmony and dynamics unchanged.
finaleClOne = { e''2.~(\ff e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 f''8 a''8 | c'''2 b''2) \breathe | e''2.~( e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 c''4 | b'2 c''2) \breathe | R1*2 | a'8(\f\< c'' e'' a'') e''( c'' a' e') | a'8( c'' e'' a'') e''( c'' a' e'') | f''8(\ff\< d'' aes' f') aes'( d'' f'') r8 | f''8( d'' aes' f') aes'( d'' f'' d'') | fis''8( es'' c'' aes') c''( es'' fis'') r8 | g''8( f'' d'' b') g'( b' d'' g'') | g''1\fff~ | g''2.\> r4\! | r8\ff g''8( e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | r8 g''8( e'''4.->) d'''8( c'''8 b''8 | c'''4->) r4 r2 | c''2->( g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | d'4( g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | e''2-> es''2 | f''1-> | c''4->\< g'8-. g'8-. c''4-. e''4->~( | e''4 d''8 des''8 c''4-.) r4 | c''4-> r8 b'8 c''4-> r4 | e''1->\fff~ | e''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c185 (Gould PDF 66-67): at 257-262 Cl 2's whole notes stood on the up-stem of Cl 1's first eighth (read as a two-note eighth chord); they are set to the left, clear of the stem.
% c395 (Gould PDF 56-57): shift -1.0 instead of -1.3, still clear of Cl 1's head; the 1.3 shift cost each bar 0.6 space that page 15 needs for the barline clearance.
finaleClTwo = { e'2.~(\ff e'8 d'8 | c'4 a'2-- g'4 | f'4. e'8 d'4 f'8 a'8 | c''2 b'2) \breathe | e''2.~( e''8 d''8 | c''4 a''2-- g''4 | f''4. e''8 d''4 c''4 | b'2 c''2) \breathe | R1*2 | \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #-1.0 e''1\f\<~ | e''1 \breathe | d''1\ff\<~ | d''1 | c''1 | b'1 | \revert NoteColumn.force-hshift c''1\fff~ | c''2.\> r4\! | \partCombineApart c''2->(\ff g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | e''2->( b'4. c''8 | d''4-.) c''8( b'8 a'4-.) g'4-. | \partCombineAutomatic c''2->( g'4. a'8 | bes'4-.) a'8( g'8 f'4-.) e'4-. | d'4( g'8 a'8 b'4 d''4) | a''2-> g''4( f''4) \breathe | \once \override NoteColumn.force-hshift = #0 c''2-> a'2 | aes'1-> | g'1~\< | g'1 | e'4-> r8 d'8 e'4-> r4 | c''1->\fff~ | c''4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c345 (MOLA PDF 5: logical cues during long periods of rest, audible and obvious, in the reading key; Example 4, PDF 10): part-only cue, tag 'part; score and MIDI unchanged.
finaleBassCl = { R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vc" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vc." } } | a,1\f\<~ | a,1 | f,1\ff\<~ | f,1 \breathe | aes,1 | g,1 | c1\fff~ | c4 r4 r2 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1*4 | c1->\ff | c1 | c1~\< | c1 | R1\! | c1->~\fff | c4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c460 (Sebesky ch. 1, PDF 19-20, Ex. 3/3A: do not harmonise a melody the same way twice; a substitution gives a phrase a lift and reaches the same resting place): 270 restated 266's F1 continuation (B-flat A G F E) over the same C-F sonority; now Bn 1 A3 and the violas D4 join Tpt 3's F4 on 270.2-4, ii7 over the C pedal, so 269-272 run I - ii7 - V - IV6/4 into 273. No horn or trombone rest is filled (265-272 protection); 266 unchanged.
finaleBsnOne = { r8 g,8(\ff e4.->) d8( des8 c8 | c4--) b,8( c8) a,4.-> g,8 | r8 g,8( f4.->) e8( d8 c8) | c8-. d-. e-. d-. g4.-> f8 | r8 g,8( e4.->) d8( c8 b,8 | c4) e8( d8) a4.-> g8 | d8( e8 f2.->) | g2.-> r4 | R1*2 | c'2.(\f\< d'4 | g'4. f'8 e'4 d'4) | d'1\ff\<~ | d'1 \breathe | c'1 | b1 | c'1\fff~ | c'4 r4 r2 | \partCombineApart c'2->(\ff g4. a8 | bes4-.) a8( g8 f4-.) e4-. | e'2->( b4. c'8 | d'4-.) c'8( b8 a4-.) g4-. | r8\ff g8( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) a2.-> | r8 g8( e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) f'2.-> | \partCombineAutomatic e'2-> es'2 | f'1-> | e'1~\< | e'1 | R1 | c'1->~\fff | c'4 r4 r2\fermata | }
finaleBsnTwo = { R1*8 | R1*2 | a,1\f\<~ | a,1 | f,1\ff\<~ | f,1 \breathe | aes,1 | g,1 | c1\fff~ | c4 r4 r2 | c4->\ff r4 r2 | c4-> r4 r2 | c4-> r4 r2 | c4-> r4 r2 | c4->\ff r4 r2 | c4-> r4 r2 | c4-> r4 r2 | a1-> | c1-> | c1-> | c1~\< | c1 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4-> r4 | c1->\fff~ | c4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c099 (Casanelles, Hyperorchestra ch. 6, PDF 274: the major triad is the harmonic series above the lowest fundamental):
% 263 and 278 Cbn C1 (was C2), as at the arrival 223, so the cadence (G1-C1, with Tpt 1 B-C) and the last chord have the same depth as 223; Cb, tuba stay on C2.
