% c046 (Adler PDF 292: frequent rests for the brass; adler2 PDF 6, 8): Tbn 1-2 rest 265-268 (they played every bar 247-279, about 43 s at f-fff); the F1 head's lower octave stays in the cellos
% c070 (cyclic lens; cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 6 and mvt1.md 'kept for the finale': a fast ff full-brass combination of the two heads in C major; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 72:
% diatonic major melodies, rousing and triumphant, suit the brass): 247-254 Tbn 1-2 a2 play movement I's first-theme head two octaves below Fl 1-2/Vln I-II, so both heads are in the brass
% (S head in Tpt 1-2, Hn 1-2). Two octaves down avoids both objections of c059 (octave above = E7; octave below crossed the trumpets' S head). Held f harmony stays in Hn 3-4, B.tbn, tuba.
% c071 (melody; Adler ch. 11, PDF 28-29 Ex. XI-13 1812: theme in trombones, bassoons and low strings below the counterpoint; PDF 19-20 Hindemith: register decides the colour): 247-254 the P head in Tbn 1-2 moves down an octave (G2-A3, three octaves below the violins) and Bsn 1 and cellos join it, so it no longer sits in the S head's octave (Hn 1-2, Cl, Vla E4-C5).
% c124 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, PDF 147: in d'Indy's unison tutti the trombones are marked ppp 'so as not to dominate'; PDF 151: Mozart weights the octave the listener must hear as the theme; PDF 154: brass doubling a figure's eighths adds 'too much weight'; PDF 150: Barber builds a crescendo by adding brass, and the low brass entering on the last phrase gives it finality): Tbn 1-2 rest 269-270 and enter ff at 271 with Tpt 1-2 (were ff from 269), so at 269-270, while Tpt 1-2 rest, movement I's head (Hn 3-4, Bn 1, Vla, Vc) no longer covers F1 in winds and violins with four ff brass, and the 269-271 crescendo of Tpt 3, tuba and timpani ends with both brass pairs entering together at 271 (re-entry ff, Rimsky PDF 136, as c123 required).
% c124 (Adler ch. 15, PDF 154: held and struck notes under a tutti carry its harmony): 272 timpani C instead of G on beat 2.5 (c4 r8 c8 c4 r4): the G struck ff with Tpt 3's A4 under the IV6/4 (F-A over C, since c122) was a foreign bass note; the pulse keeps its rhythm.
finaleTbnOne = { \clef bass r8 g,8(\f e4.->) d8( des8 c8 | c4--) b,8( c8) a,4.-> g,8 | r8 g,8( f4.->) e8( d8 c8) | c8-. d-. e-. d-. g4.-> f8 | r8 g,8( e4.->) d8( c8 b,8 | c4) e8( d8) a4.-> g8 | d8( e8 f2.->) | g2.-> r4 | g1\f~ | g1 | e'1~\< | e'1 \breathe | d'1\ff~ | d'1 | c'1\< | b1 | c'1\fff~ | c'4 r4 r2 | R1*4 | R1*2 | r8 g8(\ff e'4.->) d'8( c'8 b8 | c'4->) r4 r2 | c'2-> a2 | aes1-> | g1~\< | g1 | c'4-> r8 d'8 c'4-> r4 | c'1->\fff~ | c'4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c177 (texture; Adler ch. 8, PDF 257 Ex. VIII-25: in a first inversion the third in the bass is doubled toward the bottom, keeping the chord open; PDF 257: unison overlapping only gives a stronger pitch): 255-256 Tbn 2 E3 with the bass trombone (was G3 in unison with Hn 1, Tbn 1 and, at 255, Hn 2, two octaves under the chorale's G4), then C3 at 257 by a third instead of a fifth leap. Chords I6 and iii and the chorale line unchanged.
finaleTbnTwo = { \clef bass r8 g,8(\f e4.->) d8( des8 c8 | c4--) b,8( c8) a,4.-> g,8 | r8 g,8( f4.->) e8( d8 c8) | c8-. d-. e-. d-. g4.-> f8 | r8 g,8( e4.->) d8( c8 b,8 | c4) e8( d8) a4.-> g8 | d8( e8 f2.->) | g2.-> r4 | e1\f~ | e1 | c1~\< | c1 \breathe | aes1\ff~ | aes1 | es1\< | g1 | g1\fff~ | g4 r4 r2 | R1*4 | R1*2 | r8 g,8(\ff e4.->) d8( c8 b,8 | c4->) r4 r2 | g2-> fis2 | f1-> | e1~\< | e1 | g4-> r8 g8 g4-> r4 | g1->\fff~ | g4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c393 (form; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 136: after a long rest the trombones and tuba re-enter pp or ff, a forte re-entry is less successful, and a group silent for some time gains fresh interest; PDF 125 and ch. III PDF 85: the bass doubled in octaves; c321's own point: no held brass at F2's pitches): 255-256 the bass trombone, back after 20 bars' rest (235-254) for the chorale's last statement, enters ff on E2 with the tuba (was f on E3, in unison with Tbn 2 and on the cellos' and violas' E3 in F2 at 255.4); it then keeps the tuba's upper octave (A2, F2, A flat 2, G2, C3) at ff, crescendo from 259 to fff at 263 as before. Pitch classes, Tbn 2's E3, the chorale top line and 259-263 unchanged.
% c454 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 85, 87, 89: harmonic notes must not be lacking in the upper parts; bass rarely more than an octave below the next part): at 259-260 the bass trombone takes C3, the seventh of the half-diminished ii6/5, which only Ob 2 held, and fills the F2-A-flat3 gap; tuba, contrabassoon, bass clarinet, Bn 2, cellos and basses keep the F bass.
finaleBassTbn = { R1*8 | e,1\ff~ | e,1 | a,1~ | a,1 | c1~ | c1 \breathe | aes,1\< | g,1 | c1\fff~ | c4 r4 r2 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1*4 | c1->\ff | c1 | c1~\< | c1 | R1\! | c1->~\fff | c4 r4 r2\fermata | }
finaleTuba = { c,1\ff | d,1 | f,,1 | g,,1 \breathe | e,2 c,2 | f,,1 | d,1 | g,,1 | e,1\ff~ | e,1 | a,,1~ | a,,1 \breathe | f,,1~ | f,,1 | aes,,1\< | g,,1 | c,1\fff~ | c,4 r4 r2 | c,4->\f r4 r2 | c,4-> r4 r2 | c,4-> r4 r2 | c,4-> r4 r2 | c,4->\< r4 r2 | c,4-> r4 r2 | c,4->\ff r4 r2 | c,4-> r4 r2 | c,1-> | c,1 | c,1~\< | c,1 | c,4-> r8 g,,8 c,4-> r4 | c,1->\fff~ | c,4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c314 (orchestration; Adler ch. 15, adler2 PDF 184: Bloch marks the timpani and bass-drum rolls piano and then crescendo, because a fortissimo roll would obliterate the rest of the orchestra and change the emphasis; PDF 164-165: a chord must be distributed so that its important notes stay under control; PDF 184 (Schoenberg): the principal voice is marked louder than the accompanying ones):
%   273 the timpani's C roll under the closing chain G-F sharp-F-E starts f (was ff carried from 271), as the cellos' and basses' C pedal (c119), and still crescendos from 275 to the fff at 278.
finaleTimp = { c4\f r8 c8 c4 r8 c8 | R1 | c4 r8 c8 c4 r8 c8 | g,4 r8 g,8 g,4 r8 g,8 | c4 r8 c8 c4 r8 c8 | R1 | R1 | g,4 r8 g,8 g,4 r8 g,8 | R1^\markup \small \italic "hard sticks" | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | aes,1:32\f\< | g,1:32 | c1:32\fff~ | c4 r4 r2 | c4->\f r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4->\< r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4->\ff r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c4-> r8 c8 c4 r4 | c1:32->\f~ | c1:32~ | c1:32\<~ | c2:32 g,4 g,8 g,8 | c4-> r8 g,8 c4 r4 | c1:32\fff~ | c4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c330 (cyclic lens; Casanelles ch. 5, PDF 200, 227, 235): 276.3-277.1 the timpani leave the roll for the scherzo's closing motive x
% (scherzo Timp 363-371, b,4 b,8 b,8 e,4, 5-5-5-1) on G-G-G-C, in the rest after the theme-A quotation; the finale's earlier motive x (9, 143-145, 178)
% stays on G, so its resolution to the tonic is kept for the last bars; it lands on 277's pulse (movement I 179-180).
% c029 (holistic, Dvořák 9/iv; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 115-116, 118): 275-276 the scherzo's theme A head (scherzo 13: e4 b8 b8 e4 | g2 fis8 f8 | e4)
% transposed to C and turned to major (E flat -> E natural), note values kept and rebarred across the 2/2 bars: Tpt 1-2 and Cl 1 C5, Fl 1 C6, Hn 1-2 C4,
% over the strings' tremolo and the held wood-wind chord. 279: no accent on the tied quarter (a tied note cannot be re-attacked).
% c033 (form; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 127, 130): 255-256 the wood-wind rest after the ff heads; they re-enter at 257 p and crescendo to fff at 263 under the ff brass
% chorale (amplification: the second group enters p and reaches its crescendo faster), so 255-262 is one rising span instead of an ff plateau with a hairpin from 259.
% c028: no crash at 247 or 251; the cymbals are held back for 263, the last cadence (Adler II PDF 37: save a colour for a specific event)
% c030 (cyclic lens; Casanelles ch. 5, PDF 227, 235, 243: a reserved colour keeps its meaning only if it stays rare): no crashes at 265-273;
% the cymbals sound three times in the movement, at the arrival (223), the last cadence (263) and the final chord (278), as finale.md states.
% c050 (cyclic lens; Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 125-126, transference: the last note of one part coincides with the first note of the next; PDF 128, law of register;
% Steinberg's preface PDF 20: a theme's colour belongs to it): 275-276 Hn 1-2 complete the chain G-F sharp-F-E on E4 (held, as the scherzo's Hn 1 holds G sharp 4 after
% B-A sharp-A-G sharp), instead of leaving F4 for the quotation's C4; Vln I (the scherzo theme's own instrument, scherzo Vln I 13) takes the quotation at C6
% with Fl 1, its held C6 becoming the quotation's first note; the quotation is now Fl 1 + Vln I (C6), Tpt 1-2 + Cl 1 (C5).
% c039 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 132-134: passing notes against held harmony in the same octave and family sound harsher the slower they move): at 276 (Largamente) Hn 3, Vln I and Vln II leave the held C in the octaves of the quotation's D-D flat (Hn 3 G4, Vln I E5-G5, Vln II G4-E5); 279 timpani: no accent on the tied note (completes c029).
% c146 (Adler ch. 12, PDF 80: show exactly how long each note may ring): 278 the bass drum is tied into 279 with the cymbals, so both stop with the orchestra on 279 beat 2 (it stopped on 279 beat 1 while the cymbals and the orchestra held); the 279 cymbal note keeps the circled head of 278.
% c275 (engraving; Gould, Behind Bars PDF 25-26: the circle round a crossed notehead replaces the white space of a minim or semibreve only, so a black-note duration takes a plain cross; PDF 13-14 and 52 (double-stemmed writing, upper part up-stems, lower part down-stems)): 279 the cymbal quarter takes a plain cross (the tie from the circled semibreve at 278 shows it is the same stroke); 278-279 cymbals and bass drum are two voices, cymbal stem up and bass drum stem down with its tie below the line, as at 223, instead of one chord whose bass-drum tie was drawn above the cymbal. MIDI unchanged.
% c204 (orchestration; Rimsky-Korsakov, appendix of single tutti chords, PDF 507 chords 17 and 18: the harp runs up into the final tutti chord by glissando or arpeggio; ch. IV, PDF 134, artificial effects: long harp glissandi are resonant and brilliant; PDF 136: a group silent for some time gains fresh interest on its return; ch. I, PDF 51: plucked strings are heard apart from sustained groups): the harp, silent since its étouffez at 243, plays one C-major glissando C4-G6 ff on 277 beat 4, the only quarter rest of the close (only the basses' eighths sound), and lands with the orchestra on 278 (G6 with the flutes and Vln I; C2-C3 in the left hand), l.v. Pedals are still all naturals from 235 (diagram restated, score only); part-only Vln I cue at 275-276 before the entry after 33 bars' rest (MOLA PDF 5). No other part changes; G6 stays below the C7 reserved for 223.
% c249 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 134, artificial effects: long harp glissandi are more resonant and brilliant than short ones, and their notes need not match the orchestra's; Ex. 275, PDF 441-442: the glissandi in Pan Voyevoda sweep for half a bar each, under the sounding orchestra, with a crescendo): 277 the glissando starts with the orchestra's C on beat 3, from G3 (a half note, three octaves to G6) instead of a quarter from C4 on beat 4, f < into the ff of 278; top note, tuning, landing chord and every other part unchanged.
% c256 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 47: a forte scale glissando through the middle and lower strings gives "a discordant medley of sound owing to the length of time
%   the strings continue to vibrate" and is only an embellishment, while glissandi on chords "enharmonically obtained" are free of that reservation at every dynamic; PDF 46: the low and middle
%   strings ring on): 277 the glissando G3-G6 runs on the pentatonic chord C-D-E-G-A, B set to B sharp (= C) and F to F flat (= E), so no B or F rings into the fff C chord at 278 with the
%   laissez-vibrer G6 and the C-G-C chord. Two pedal moves (B right foot, F left foot) in the harp's 30 bars of rest; endpoints, rhythm and dynamics unchanged.
finaleHarpR = { R1*28 | \tag #'score { R1*2 } \tag #'part { \cueDuring "vlnOne" #UP { R1*2^\markup \tiny "Vln. I" } } | r2 g2-\tag #'part ^\markup \small "gliss. C D E G A (B♯ = C, F♭ = E)"\glissando | g'''4\laissezVibrer r4 r2 | r4 r4 r2\fermata | }
% c346 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 46): 278 "non arpegg.", the left-hand chord lands with the tutti on the beat
% c386 (Adler ch. 4, adler1 PDF 117, sons étouffés; movement I damps its last harp chord C2-G2-C3 at I 182): the harp damps on 279.2, when the tutti releases, so its low C2-G2-C3 does not ring into the closing fermata
finaleHarpL = { R1*30 | r2 s2\f\<-\tag #'score ^\markup \small \harp-pedal #"--v|-^--" | <c, g, c>4\ff_\markup \small \italic "non arpegg."\laissezVibrer r4 r2 | r4 r4-\tweak self-alignment-X #CENTER -\tweak extra-offset #'(0.6 . 0) _\markup \italic "étouffez" r2\fermata | }
finalePerc = \drummode { R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | \tweak style #'xcircle cymc1->\fff | R1 | R1*12 | R1 | << { \tweak style #'xcircle cymc1->^\fff~ | cymc4 r4 r2\fermata } \\ { bd1->_\fff~ | bd4 s4 s2 } >> | }

% c206 (Adler ch. 4, PDF 114: three-note chords per hand spread wide are the most effective; PDF 117: every glissando states its tuning): 278 the harp LH plays C2-G2-C3, movement I's last harp chord (I 182), not the bare octave; the part prints the glissando's tuning at 277 (the score has the pedal diagram).
